ow the statement are two small poset diagrams labeled R1 and R2. R1 is a 4-element shape with one top node connected down-left to a middle node, which connects down to a bottom node, and the top node also connects down-right to a right node. R2 is a 5-element zigzag shape with two top nodes, three bottom nodes, and alternating edges forming a W-like chain.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npermutation pattern — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/permutation%20pattern)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 2026-10-20 ... inversion sequence lehmer code permutation pattern poset sequence permutation ... Motivated by a recent conjecture of Bsila, Cox, Hugo, Styron and Zhuang concerning fixed points and pixed points on pattern-avoiding permutations, we prove a bivariate refinement involving descent statistics. Given a set of permutations $Π$, let $\\mathfrak{S}_n(Π)$ denote the set of permutations in the symmetric group $\\mathfrak{S}_n$ that avoid every element of $Π$ in the sense of pattern avoidance.\n\n2026-09-13 v3\n\nSergey Kitaev, Philip B. Zhang\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nDescent generating polynomials for ($n-3$)- and ($n-4$)-stack-sortable (pattern-avoiding) permutations\n\nIn this paper, we find distribution of descents over $(n-3)$- and $(n-4)$-stack-sortable permutations in terms of Eulerian polynomials. Our results generalize the enumeration results by Claesson, Dukes, and Steingr\\'ımsson on $(n-3)$- and $(n-4)$-stack-sortable permutations. Moreover, we find distribution of descents on $(n-2)$-, $(n-3)$- and $(n-4)$-stack-sortable permutations that avoid any given pattern of length 3, which extends known results in the literature on distribution of descents over pattern-avoiding 1- and 2-stack-sortable permutations. Our distribution results also give enumeration of $(n-2)$-, $(n-3)$- and $(n-4)$-stack-sortable permutations avoiding any pattern of length 3. One of our conjectures links our work to stack-sorting with restricted stacks, and the other conjecture states that 213-avoiding permutations sortable with $t$ stacks are equinumerous with 321-avoiding permutations sortable with $t$ stacks for any $t$.\n\n2026-05-30\n\nYao Dong, Chao Xu\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA Refinement of the Fixed--Pixed Points Equidistribution on restricted Permutations\n\nMotivated by a recent conjecture of Bsila, Cox, Hugo, Styron and Zhuang concerning fixed points and pixed points on pattern-avoiding permutations, we prove a bivariate refinement involving descent statistics. Given a set of permutations $Π$, let $\\mathfrak{S}_n(Π)$ denote the set of permutations in the symmetric group $\\mathfrak{S}_n$ that avoid every element of $Π$ in the sense of pattern avoidance. For each set $Π$ appearing in their conjecture, we show that the pairs of statistics $(\\mathrm{des},\\mathrm{fix})$ and $(\\mathrm{ides},\\mathrm{pix})$ are equidistributed over $\\mathfrak{S}_n(Π)$. Our proof is based on explicit ordinary generating functions for the corresponding pattern-avoiding classes.\n\ndescent statistic group pattern avoidance pattern-avoiding permutation pattern symmetric group permutation--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nco.combinatorics - Pattern Avoidance in Poset Permutations - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/212683/pattern-avoidance-in-poset-permutations?r=31)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Pattern Avoidance in Poset Permutations ... I am not sure if it is appropriate to use MathOverflow to publicize a conjecture, but I think this is an interesting question and I have no real ideas of how to solve it. ... We say $\\sigma$ contains a permutation $\\pi = (\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_k)$ (which we call a pattern) on the $k$-element poset $Q$ if there is some subsequence $\\sigma_{i_1},\\ldots,\\sigma_{i_k}$ with $i_1 < \\cdots < i_k$ order-isomorphic to $\\pi$: i.e., $\\sigma_{i_r} < \\sigma_{i_s}$ iff $\\pi_{r} < \\pi_{s}$ for all $1 \\leq r,s \\leq k$.\n    9. # Pattern Avoidance in Poset Permutations\n\nAsked 10 years, 11 months ago\n\nModified 10 years, 11 months ago\n\nViewed 377 times\n\nI am not sure if it is appropriate to use MathOverflow to publicize a conjecture, but I think this is an interesting question and I have no real ideas of how to solve it.\n\nA permutation on a $n$-element poset $P$ is a list $\\sigma = (\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_n)$ of the elements of $P$ in some order. We say $\\sigma$ contains a permutation $\\pi = (\\pi_1,\\ldots,\\pi_k)$ (which we call a pattern) on the $k$-element poset $Q$ if there is some subsequence $\\sigma_{i_1},\\ldots,\\sigma_{i_k}$ with $i_1 < \\cdots < i_k$ order-isomorphic to $\\pi$: i.e., $\\sigma_{i_r} < \\sigma_{i_s}$ iff $\\pi_{r} < \\pi_{s}$ for all $1 \\leq r,s \\leq k$. If $\\sigma$ does not contain $\\pi$ then $\\sigma$ avoids $\\pi$. We write $\\pi = \\pi_1\\cdots\\pi_k$ for a pattern instead of using parentheses. Let $\\mathrm{Av}_P(\\pi)$ denote the number of permutations on $P$ that avoid $\\pi$.\n\nIn http://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5718 Morgan Weiler and I show that $\\mathrm{Av}_P(132) \\leq \\mathrm{Av}_P(123)$ for all finite posets $P$. (Here $1,2,3$ are elements of the chain poset $1 < 2 < 3$.)\n\nQuestion: Is it the case that $\\mathrm{Av}_P(\\{1\\}\\{1,2\\}\\{2\\}) \\leq \\mathrm{Av}_P(\\{1\\}\\{2\\}\\{1,2\\})$ for all finite posets $P$? (Here $\\{1\\},\\{2\\},\\{1,2\\}$ are ordered by containment.) This is the case for all posets on seven or fewer elements.\n\n  * co.combinatorics\n  * posets\n  * pattern-avoidance\n\nasked Jul 31, 2015 at 1:40\n\nSam Hopkins♦\n\n26.6k 6 6 gold badges 113 113 silver badges 210 210 bronze badges\n\n  * The article mentioned above is published here: link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11083-015-9367-7.\n\n–  Sam Hopkins ♦--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nparallelogram polyomino — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/parallelogram%20polyomino)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This paper establishes connections between EW-tableaux and parallelogram polyominoes by using recent research regarding the sandpile model on the complete bipartite graph. ... In this work we extend a bijection between ordered trees and parallelogram polyominoes in order to compute the generating function of periodic parallelogram polyominoes with respect to the height, the width and the intrinsic thickness, a new statistic unrelated to the existing statistics on parallelogram polyominoes.\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n## parallelogram polyomino ↗ reference\n\n27 papers tagged with this keyword\n\n2026-04-14\n\nJean-Luc Baril, Aubrey Blecher, José Luis Ramírez\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nEnumerations and Bijections for Stanley Polyominoes\n\nStanley polyominoes are a subclass of parallelogram polyominoes in which each row begins strictly to the right of the beginning of the previous row and ends strictly to the right of the end of the previous row. In this paper, we derive generating functions for Stanley polyominoes based on the numbers of columns and rows, area, semiperimeter, and numbers of interior points and edges. We also establish combinatorial connections through bijections with other combinatorial structures such as Dyck paths, skew Ferrer diagrams, and peakless Motzkin paths. As a byproduct, we answer the open question of finding a bijection between parallelogram polyominoes of area $n$ and coin fountains with $n$ coins in the even-numbered rows and $n-k$ coins in the odd-numbered rows.\nAn unexpected application leads to a refinement of a result of Bousquet-Mélou and Viennot on the width-height-area generating function of parallelogram polyominoes. Finally, we exhibit the relation of the arising alternating tableaux to plane partitions of strip shapes.\n\ndyck path lattice path non-intersecting lattice path orthogonal polynomial parallelogram polyomino plane partition polyomino sequence polynomial partition lattice\n\n2020-11-18\n\nMichele D'Adderio, Alessandro Iraci, Anna Vanden Wyngaerd\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nDecorated Dyck paths, polyominoes, and the Delta conjecture\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe discuss the combinatorics of decorated Dyck paths and decorated parallelogram polyominoes, extending to the decorated case the main results of both [Haglund 2004] and [Aval et al. 2014]. This settles in particular the cases $\\langle\\cdot,e_{n-d}h_d\\rangle$ and $\\langle\\cdot,h_{n-d}h_d\\rangle$ of the Delta conjecture of Haglund, Remmel and Wilson (2018). Along the way, we introduce some new statistics, formulate some new conjectures, prove some new identities of symmetric functions, and answer a few open problems in the literature (e.g. from [Haglund et al. 2018], [Zabrocki 2016], [Aval et al. 2015]). The main technical tool is a new identity in the theory of Macdonald polynomials that extends a theorem of Haglund in [Haglund 2004]. This is an edited merge of arXiv:1712.08787 and arXiv:1709.08736\n\naffine permutation dyck path parallelogram polyomino polyomino permutation\n\n2016-11-11\n\nAdrien Boussicault, Patxi Laborde-Zubieta\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nPeriodic Parallelogram Polyominoes\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nA periodic parallelogram polyomino is a parallelogram polyomino such that we glue the first and the last column. In this work we extend a bijection between ordered trees and parallelogram polyominoes in order to compute the generating function of periodic parallelogram polyominoes with respect to the height, the width and the intrinsic thickness, a new statistic unrelated to the existing statistics on parallelogram polyominoes. Moreover we define a rotation over periodic parallelogram polyominoes, which induces a partitioning in equivalent classes called strips. We also compute the generating function of strips using the theory of Pólya.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch (https://mocowe.github.io/website/research.html)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago;   * Pattern avoidance in poset permutations (2015), with Sam Hopkins. We extend the concept of permutation pattern avoidance to partially ordered sets.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npolyomino — arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/polyomino)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... We give a bijection with labeled Dyck paths that provides combinatorial proofs of these formulas and translates natural statistics on Fibonacci polyominoes into peaks, returns, and related statistics on Dyck paths. ... In this paper we define a novel product on polyominoes called the tensor product of polyominoes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeminars :: math.ucdavis.edu (https://mass.math.ucdavis.edu/research/seminars?talk_id=3569)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; A recent paper of Dukes and Le Borgne studied two statistics on parallelogram polyominoes-- two nonintersecting paths, each composed of north and east steps and bounded by a rectangular m x n bounding box.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb page of Mathilde Bouvel (https://user.math.uzh.ch/bouvel/publications/)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ArXiv, JCT-A ... Slicings of parallelogram polyominoes: Catalan, Schr�der, Baxter, and other sequences.\nWith Nick Beaton, Veronica Guerrini and Simone Rinaldi.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nparallelogram polyomino — 